---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContentCTA from '../components/content/ContentCTA.astro';
import { marketingPages } from '../data/marketing-content';
import '../styles/content.css';
const meta = marketingPages.find((page) => page.path === '/about')!;
---
<Layout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} active="about" breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about' }]}>
  <div class="content-page container">
    <header class="content-about-hero"><span class="content-kicker">ABOUT FETCHX</span><h1>The web is complicated.<br/><span>Your access layer<br/>should be clear.</span></h1><p class="content-lead">FetchX is a web access API for applications that need to work with web sources.</p></header>
    <section class="content-about-purpose"><span class="content-kicker">THE TECHNICAL PURPOSE</span><div><h2>Make source access<br/>something you can reason about.</h2><p>Web applications need more than a response body. They need to know which source answered, which route was used, whether an operation may have reached the source, and what can safely happen next.</p><p>FetchX puts those questions into an explicit contract: approved source profiles, deliberate routing, durable operation keys, retained response evidence and conservative spending reservations.</p><p>The caller keeps control of discovery, parsing, scheduling and application acceptance. That boundary lets the transport layer and the data pipeline be evaluated independently.</p></div></section>
    <section class="content-benefits content-about-values" aria-label="Engineering principles"><article><span class="content-number">01</span><h2>Explicit choices</h2><p>Destinations, profile versions, proxy class and country are visible inputs. Permission and budget stay attached to the project.</p></article><article><span class="content-number">02</span><h2>Durable evidence</h2><p>Lost responses need recovery, not guesswork. Operation keys and retained captures preserve what is known about a source request.</p></article><article><span class="content-number">03</span><h2>Honest boundaries</h2><p>Network success, extracted fields and application acceptance are separate claims. Proposed capabilities are identified as previews.</p></article></section>
    <section class="content-about-open"><div><span class="content-kicker">API DOCUMENTATION</span><h2>Read the docs.<br/>See how requests work.</h2></div><div><p>The FetchX documentation covers the HTTP API, reviewed browser workflows and runtime limitations. Source-specific configuration and credentials remain private to the operator.</p><p>This website is a product design preview. AI-assisted request diagnosis and strategy selection describe a proposed direction, while the API documentation describes the current implementation.</p><div class="content-actions"><a class="content-text-link" href="/docs">Read the API documentation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="content-text-link" href="/contact">Contact FetchX <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section>
    <ContentCTA/>
  </div>
</Layout>
